Experiment (a): Working with basic bash commands.
COMMANDS:

1. pwd : It tells us the complete location of the directory where we are currently working.
Options:

* pwd -L - Displays the logical path of the current directory.
Example : $ pwd -L -> /home/user/College.
* pwd -P - Displays the physical path of the current working directory.
Example: $ pwd -P -> /home/user/College.
* pwd --help - Gives information about the command and explains its available options.
Example : $ pwd --help -> pwd [OPTION]...

2. ls : It is used to display the files and directories available at a particular location.
Options:

* ls -l - Shows a long listing containing detailed file information.
Example: -rw-r--r-- 1 student staff 2048 Sep 10 11:20 report.txt.
* ls -a : Shows all contents present, including the hidden items.
Example: . ... profile .config main.c.
* ls -lt : Shows detailed information arranged according to modification time.
Example: 11:20 report.txt -> 9:15 main.c -> 8:30 notes.txt.

3. cd : It is used to shift or update the current folder location.
Options:

* cd .. : Navigates backward to the main parent folder.
Example : ~/Projects/Code -> ~/Projects (moves one level up).
* cd [directory name] : Navigates into the specified folder.
Example: $cd Desktop -> /Desktop$ (opens Desktop folder).
* cd : Directly redirects the workspace to the user home folder.
Example : /var/www/html -> cd -> /home/user.

4. mkdir : It is used to build or set up new directories.
Options:

* mkdir -v [name] - Displays a message confirming the folder creation.
Example: $ mkdir -v LabWork -> mkdir: created directory 'LabWork'.
* mkdir -p [name] - Creates nested parent directories if they are missing.
Example: $ mkdir -p Course/Sem1/Labs.
* mkdir -m [mode] [name] - Grants specific access modes to the new directory.
Example: $ mkdir -m 755 Storage.

---

5. rmdir : It is used to delete folders that do not contain any files.
Options:

* rmdir -p [name] - Erases the target folder along with its parent paths.
Example: $ rmdir -p Course/Sem1/Labs.
* rmdir -v [name] - Erases the folder and outputs a success status message.
Example: $ rmdir -v LabWork -> rmdir: removing directory, 'LabWork'.

6. rm : It is used to remove files as well as folder structures.
Options:

* rm -i [file name] - Requests confirmation before performing the deletion.
Example: $ rm -i test.txt -> rm: remove regular file 'test.txt'? y
* rm -r [file name] - Deletes directories along with their subfolders recursively.
Example: $ rm -r backupData.
* rm -f [file name] - Removes items forcefully without prompting the user.
Example: $ rm -f temp.log

7. touch : It is used to generate blank files or update timestamps.
Options:

* touch -a [file name] - Modifies only the last access time of the file.
Example: $ touch -a code.py
* touch -m [file name] - Modifies only the last edit time of the file.
Example: $ touch -m style.css
* touch [file name] - Creates a completely new empty file.
Example: $ touch file1.txt

8. cp : It is used to make copies of files and directories.
Options:

* cp -i [name][name] - Asks for permission before replacing an existing file.
Example: $ cp -i file1.txt copy.txt -> cp: overwrite 'copy.txt'? y
* cp -v [name][name] - Shows step-by-step information during the copying process.
Example: $ cp -v file1.txt Docs/ -> 'file1.txt' -> 'Docs/file1.txt'.
* cp -r [name][name] - Duplicates directory contents recursively to a new place.
Example: $ cp -r Audio Music

---

9. mv : It is used to relocate items or rename them.
Options:

* mv -i [name][name] - Displays a warning prompt prior to overwriting target files.
Example: $ mv -i test.c final.c -> mv: overwrite 'final.c'? y
* mv -v [name][name] - Displays details about the items shifted or renamed.
Example: $ mv -v test.c Docs/ -> renamed 'test.c' -> 'Docs/test.c'.
* mv -f [name][name] - Forces file relocation without asking for approval.
Example: $ mv -f config.json Settings/

10. head : It is used to print the top lines of a file.
Options:

* head -n [number] [name] - Displays the requested number of top lines.
Example: $ head -n 3 data.csv -> Line 1, Line 2, Line 3.
* head -c [number] [name] - Displays the specified initial byte count.
Example: $ head -c 10 data.csv -> first 10 bytes of file.

11. tail : It is used to print the bottom lines of the file.
Options:

* tail -n [number] [name] - Displays the requested number of bottom lines.
Example: $ tail -n 3 data.csv -> Line 8, Line 9, Line 10.
* tail -c [number] [name] - Displays the specified ending byte count.
Example: $ tail -c 10 data.csv -> last 10 bytes of file.
